# scan-wedged-runners.service — periodically sweep the ARC runner pool for
# runner pods that are alive to Kubernetes but permanently dead to GitHub
# (scan-wedged-runners.sh).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS: the wedged-runner condition is self-perpetuating. A dead
# runner pod suppresses its own scale set's scale-up, so the scale set cannot
# recover without an external delete — the queue simply stays blocked, for as
# long as nobody looks. The failure was found by a human noticing a merge gate
# stuck 33+ minutes (livespec-s43svm.30), which is precisely the recovery path
# that does not scale. A timer is the same pragmatic, homelab-scale answer this
# tree already uses for idempotent periodic reconciliation — see
# reapply-node-extended-resource.service and
# ../../observability/ci-runner-heartbeat.timer.
#
# MODE VALUE — READ THIS BEFORE INSTALLING. The argument below is
# MODE_PLACEHOLDER for the same reason the sibling reapply unit's capacity is:
# an un-edited copy must fail loudly rather than quietly do the wrong thing,
# and the choice between REPORTING a wedge and CLEARING it is exactly the
# decision an operator must make deliberately rather than inherit. Substitute
# either an empty string (report-only: the sweep logs findings to the journal
# and the unit fails, which is what `systemctl status` and `is-failed` then
# report) or `--clear` (the sweep deletes flagged pods).
#
# install-wedged-runner-scan.sh performs that substitution and records which
# mode was chosen; see its header for the argument in favour of --clear on this
# host, and scan-wedged-runners.sh's header for why deleting a flagged pod is
# safe by construction.
[Unit]
Description=Sweep ARC runner pods for the wedged (registration-not-found) condition
After=k3s.service
Requires=k3s.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
Environment=KUBECONFIG=/etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml
ExecStart=/usr/local/lib/ci-runner-k3s/scan-wedged-runners.sh MODE_PLACEHOLDER
